A||N||30||Seanh||Stuck Control Arm bolt?!?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||07-04-2001||02:43 PM||||Damn this sucks! I'm done with the drivers side, and its all back together. I have removed the upper arm, spring, and was removing the lower arm this morning. I started to take the rear bolt off, and I put a wrench on the nut, and turned the bolt with a bar. The bolt turned, and the nut eventually came off, but the bolt will not come out. It is apperently stuck in the sleeve, and the sleeve has broken loose from the bushing. I'm not sure what to do now. I can't believe its stuck in there very good, because none of the other bolts gave me any trouble at all. Maybe I should try and get a pry bar in between the bolt head contol arm mount and see if I can't break it loose. I guess if not its time to brake out the grinder? Advance Auto or NAPA carry the replacement bolts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||07-04-2001||03:14 PM||||I just finished grinding the heads off both lower rear control arm bolts on my 87 GT.  What a pain!  The bolts get frozen to the inner sleeve of the bushing.  I have had this happen on several cars I have worked on, always the rear bolt.<P>I ended up cutting the end of the bolt off and then grinding the head off.  You should then be able to pry the rest off the bolt and control arm from the frame tabs.<P>The bolts might still be available from GM, otherwise any good fastener supplier should be able to get them.  They are normal metric sized hardened bolts.  <P>Be sure to coat the entire bolt with antisieze when you reinstall so you never have the problem again.<P>Good luck!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||07-04-2001||03:17 PM||||theogre has a tip in the cave on this.  Try to take a hammer and punch and whack the seam of the bushing to make it open up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-04-2001||03:55 PM||||Yes, most gm dealers do carry them. They are costly though.. WHen I replaced my upper ctrl arm bolt.. it cost me $17. canadian..<P>they are a hardened metric bolt.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fiero_Freak||07-04-2001||04:13 PM||||Go to a Bolt and Fastener distributor.  The one here appropiately named Shammrock has saved my as on more than one occasion.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Left_01.jpg"> <BR>Two '87 GT's...<BR>   ...Double Trouble||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||07-04-2001||04:50 PM||||I just tried the punch & Hammer idea, to no avail. I beat on that thing for 20 minutes and it won't split for nothin. Anything else I should try before getting the grinder? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||erapp||07-04-2001||06:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>I just tried the punch & Hammer idea, to no avail. I beat on that thing for 20 minutes and it won't split for nothin. Anything else I should try before getting the grinder? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>HAVE YOU TRY'D TO SPRAY THE BOLT'S WITH A PENERTRATING OIL AND LET IT SOAK FOR A HOUR OR TWO ?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||skitime||07-04-2001||06:42 PM||||I just went through the same tihing you are going through. My advise is to pry the frame support apart on the side the head of the bolt is on.  This will give you enough room that you only have to grind through the bolt and not the head. I tried all the above and only got frustrated.  Just start grinding. I you really can't find a bolt email me because I think I have a spare if I can find it.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||07-04-2001||08:41 PM||||Sean,<P>I have two bolts from the lower control arm of an '88.....original and in great shape.   I'm not sure if they are the same as for your Fiero (I can't remember what year you have), but you are welcome to them......just pay postage and they're yours (includes the washers & nuts).<P><BR>BTW......they're about 3" long and are the "10.9" type.<P>LMK.<P> ||||reg||10
Z||000009||Tigger||07-04-2001||11:43 PM||||Yes, don't go to a dealer to find replacement bolts!<P>A local Pontiac dealer wanted to charge me $10 a pop for each bolt and about the same for each nut.  That's $80 for 4 bolts and four nuts!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  And they weren't even in stock!<P>Go to a local bolt and fastener supplier.  I got 4 bolts (correct 10.9 hardness), 4 nuts, and 8 washers for about the same as one bolt from the dealer.<P>*Muuaaahahah!!*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||07-05-2001||12:21 PM||||Ok, I broke out the grinder this morning, now I have to go get a cutting wheel for it and I'll be good to go. So whats the best way to do this? Under the car, thru the wheel well. It looks to me that I should do the back of the bolt thru the wheel well, and the head from under.<P>Hey Mike, could you just bring them with you on your trip out here tomarrow night? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000011||chester||07-05-2001||01:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Ok, I broke out the grinder this morning, now I have to go get a cutting wheel for it and I'll be good to go. So what's the best way to do this? Under the car, thru the wheel well. It looks to me that I should do the back of the bolt thru the wheel well, and the head from under.<P>Hey Mike, could you just bring them with you on your trip out here tomarrow night? Thanks!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Dude, I just did a complete suspension revamp on my 86 GT and ran into the same issue on EVERY control arm bolt.  We soaked the bolts down with Marvels Mystery oil and let it sit overnight.  The next day we whacked it with a hammer and out it came pretty as can be.  I would recommend that for you unless you are in a hurry and if so - good luck.  BTW this worked on ALL of my control arm bolts.  We tried everything on the first bolt to no avail except cutting it. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||07-05-2001||01:56 PM||||No kidding? I havent started yet, because the damn cut-off wheel I have is too small. I will try just that, and see what happens. I really don't look forward to cutting it, so I'll give this a try.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Leadfoot||07-05-2001||03:26 PM||||I just did my entire suspension with prothane and I feel your pain.  I did the whole grinding thing...I found that my dremel tool does a good job but it takes too long.  So I went to menards and bought their little torch set, a sharp cold chisel and a big hammer.  I heated up the bolt till it glowed red and then I beat it like a red-headed step child with the chisel.  I managed to snap the majority of the bolt but still had to do some grinding.  I kept a water hose handy since the rubber bushings like to catch on fire.  All I can say is have patience, you'll get it off eventually.||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||07-05-2001||07:30 PM||||When you use a center punch as I describe in my article,,,, you have to be sure you are right on the seam in the sleeve. If you aren't you could dent the sleeve into the bolt and make it even tighter...||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||07-06-2001||01:12 AM||||So what would you say is easier, heating and beating, or just grinding from the start. I have a dremal tool, so I might try that, what type of tip did you use? I don't mind if it takes all day, I'm really not looking forward to trying to fit in there with a big 'ol heavy dudy grinder. What if I heat it up, brake it off, and finish it off with the dremal?<P>It ok I guess that its taking so long. I think I'll be really happy and proud of the results, so it'll be worth it. The drivers side looks awesome. Glossy black control arms and spindle, red spring, swaybar, and tie rod ends. Everything else coated with rubber undercoating. I'll post some pictures once I get it done.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Seanh||07-06-2001||01:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Leadfoot:<BR><B>...and then I beat it like a red-headed step child with the chisel.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Interesting....The kid who caused my entire suspension rebuilt is a red-headed step child....I think I'v already punished him enough, he sounded pretty scared when I told him how much money this was gonna cost. Oh well, beating him is always an option...||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||07-06-2001||08:01 PM||||If you can hit it right on the seam with the center punch it usually splits it open a couple thosandths... you might need to do it to both end of the sleeve if its real bad. <P>It's unusual for that trick to not work. You do need a center punch with a decent point... Doesn't have to be super sharp.<P>If it doesn't work right on the seam move a tiny tad to one side. Some seams have tapered edges and don't want to play nice. the idea is to dent the edge really close or on the seam so it spreads and stays.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||07-06-2001||09:03 PM||||I just went through this with the rear control arm bolts when I installed my delrin bushings. I didn't have a grinder which would fit well enough. Instead I used a 90 degree drill and shortened drill bit to drill the heads off the bolts. Once I drilled all the way through the head and into the shank I sheared the head off with a large punch and BFH. I couldn't just drill the head all the way off because my drill only had a 3/8" chuck.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Seanh||07-07-2001||11:54 AM||||Well, maybe I'll get a different punch tonight and try again. I tried one with a point, but not very sharp point. I'm going to try everything before I have to use a grinder. I'm going to try and split it again, heat it up, beat it with a BFH for a while, maybe try and crow bar and sledge hammer if it would fit. I want to try and dremel tool, but what tip do you use? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000020||Bob Englert||07-07-2001||03:29 PM||||I hope the center punch on the seam trick works for you.  I was never able to get it to free a bolt on my cars.  <P>I don't think the dremel will have enough power to grind the bolt off, I bought an electric 4" grinder which made short work of the bolt head.  Before I had the grinder I used a sawz-all (went through about 5 blades before I got through the bolt) on a previous car.<P>Maybe there is a place in town where you can rent an electric grinder.<P>Hang in there and remember how good you will feel when you finally defeat the bolt!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000021||Seanh||07-07-2001||03:36 PM||||I have a grinder, but its a hugh mother and might be a pain to get into that small a space. I also have a smaller one, but its being an ass and won't work. I do have one big ass recipocating (sp?) saw that I think would take care of that bolt in no time, but again, its friggin' overkill for this. I guess I should just use the damn grinder and get this over with. I'm going home after work to jack around with it some more. <P>I'm getting tempted to just get my .44 and let this bolt have it. My luck it would destroy the control arm and its mounts too, if not more.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Seanh||07-07-2001||03:45 PM||||2 more questions:<P>1) Would the hospital rent me the jaws of life?<P>2)I have some old siccors I bought about 4 years ago, claimed they cut thru everything. Even cut right thru a penny with ease. Think they would put this bolt in its place?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||07-07-2001||03:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Well, maybe I'll get a different punch tonight and try again. I tried one with a point, but not very sharp point. I'm going to try everything before I have to use a grinder. I'm going to try and split it again, heat it up, beat it with a BFH for a while, maybe try and crow bar and sledge hammer if it would fit. I want to try and dremel tool, but what tip do you use? Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Don't do too much with a BFH...<BR>I bent the pivot bracket on the body doing that. I also elongated the pivot bolt hole trying to shear the head off.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Seanh||07-07-2001||03:55 PM||||Won't I have to bend the piviot brackets slightly anyway to get the arm out, once the head and shank are cut off?<P>Perhaps I should beat it with a SFH instead?<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 07-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Seanh||07-07-2001||03:57 PM||||An Idea just came to me. Air Hammer...hehehehehehe.....||||reg||1
Z||000026||Bob Englert||07-07-2001||11:32 PM||||You shouldn't have any problem bending the mount tab when you cut the bolt.  It moves pretty easily.  The rear tabs are used to position the arm, almost all the load on the control arm is carried by the from arm in the crossmember.<P>Be careful if you do use a grinder that you don't mess up the frame tab or the control arm.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000027||Tigger||07-08-2001||12:07 AM||||Grind away Seanh...  Yes, you'll have to bend the bracket away slightly but not much since you'll only need to clear a little bit of the shaft of the bolt, plus there is some give with the bushings which helps.<P>Control arm bolts just suck big time.  I spent probably 3 weekends off and on trying to soak, torch, beat, and grind two of them off on my Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Seanh||07-08-2001||01:01 PM||||Alright..This morning I had some time to mess around with it. I still didn't get it out by the way. First off, I tried the recipicating saw, it didn't seem to cut and if it did it was very slow and I only managed to bend blades...I have a 4" grinder and a 7"/8" grinder. I tried to fit the little 4" in there, but the cutoff disk still hits other objects before the bolt. I looked at the 7/8" and it looks way to big. I am having trouble understanding how to get to the damn bolt with a grinder. For the head, how do you get to it. It seems that under the car, kinda from the inside is the only way to access it and my grinder still hits other things before the bolt head. On the threaded end of the bolt I have no clue how to get to it. I see a brake line running underneath it, and there doesn't look to be enough room to get to it from the side (thru wheel well)....Well, then I tried beating it some more with a BFH and then a bigger BFH and that didn't work either (didn't expect it to, I just wanted to take my anger out on something, and seeing as I had a hammer and the bolt was right there, I decided to beat it, ya know, maybe kill 2 birds with one store, make me feel better and loosen the bolt, who knows?) The only thing I didn't try is heating it up with a tourch. The only one I have it a propane one, but I'm sure it would heat it enough to glow...after some time..<P>Well, I don't really know what I'll do now. Maybe just take the engine out and sell the car. I'm really getting pissed at it, and am quickly looseing my desire to make it drivable again.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Bob Englert||07-08-2001||07:19 PM||||Hang in there!  I know how you feel.<P>When I did my first suspension rebuild on my first 85 SE, I couldn't for the life of my figure out how to get the springs back into the control arms.  I would try for a while and get totally frustrated and would just walk away.  This went on for several months, I missed the best part of the summer driving season.  Then one fall evening I got inspired and figured out an easy and safe way using a JCW spring lowering kit to help hold the springs compressed.  After I figured it out they went in in about 1 hour!<P>Basically you want to grind the head off the bolt any way you can, at whatever angle will allow you to reach the bolt without hitting other components.  If you got the nut off the back end of the bolt can be cut outside the frame tab.  Once they are ground off on both sides, you can spread the tabs and remove the control arm.<P>It WILL be worth it when you finally get it out.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000030||Seanh||07-08-2001||07:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Basically you want to grind the head off the bolt any way you can, at whatever angle will allow you to reach the bolt without hitting other components.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That was excatly what I needed to hear, and figured that out for myself about 30 minutes ago. For some reason I had trouble conprehending that I didn't have to cut the bolt head off at a 90 degree angle. I am cutting away, just taking a brake now. Almost got the head off, then to the threaded end. <P>I was just like that a week ago, with my spring. I finally decided upon renting a spring compressor, compressed it on a bench, wired it together with 2 loops of coat hanger wire, and it went in perfectly.<P>Ok, maybe I'll keep the car now...Unless someone offers me $4K or above, then I'll sell it. Once it's done, I'm going to put a for sale sign in the window, $5000. I don't want to sell it, but if I could find someone with some money and a taste for rear-engine sports cars, willing to pay $5K for I'd sell it and buy that awesome 85GT sitting on the dealer lot here. The guy will take no less then $5500 for it, a silver 85GT in prestine condition, 70K miles, perfect inside and out. I think that price is a little outta line, but I'd pay it if I had it.||||reg||1
